Context: Section X/XI of 'Variations sur l'équation des lacets'. Grothendieck works over a base scheme S with a form G of GL(2), its derived forms SG and PG of SL(2) and PGL(2), and sections ρ, σ of PG. In the modular case these generate the image of SL2(Z) = Z/6 *_{Z/2} Z/4: ρ has trace 1 and order 6 in SL2, σ has trace 0 and σ^2 = -1. The unipotent 'lacets' are ε0 = -σ^{-1}ρ and ε1 = -ρσ^{-1}. The algebraic groups M_{ρ,σ} and M°_{ρ,σ} are group schemes built from this data. T_ρ and T_σ are the centralizer tori of ρ and σ in G, and T_0 is attached to ε0. The previous page computed the centre of M_{ρ,σ}; its integral points are Z/6 × Z/2 × Z/2, of order 24.

On this page he computes the centre of M°_{ρ,σ}. He argues that M°_{ρ,σ} is built from SG and the commutative group M°_{ρ,σ}(0) ≅ T°_{ρ,σ} ⊂ T_0 × T_ρ × T_σ. He then claims the centre is the direct product of two pieces:
- the centre μ2 of SG;
- the subgroup of T° acting trivially on SG, namely T° ∩ (1 × T_ρ × T_σ) = {(1,a,b) | det a = 1, det b = 1} ≅ ST_ρ × ST_σ.

A side note corrects the previous page's (50). There the condition was only (det a)^2 = 1; here det a = 1 is forced because (1,a,b) lies in T°.

This gives the crossed-out formula (13): Centre(M°) ≅ (1 × ST_ρ × ST_σ) × μ2. In the modular case, (14) reads Centre(M°)(Z) ≅ 1 × Z/6 × Z/4 × Z/2:
- Z/6 = norm-one units of Z[ζ6];
- Z/4 = norm-one units of Z[i];
- Z/2 = Γ(μ2) ⊂ Γ(SG).

This has order 48, and he remarks it is only twice the order 24 found for the centre of M_{ρ,σ}. A remark adds that the neutral component of the centre is ST_ρ × ST_σ, of dimension 2. Then come marginal notes 'faux!' and 'faux! centre de dim 3', and (13) is struck out. Grothendieck himself recognised that the centre is 3-dimensional, so the page's conclusion is retracted. The next page (508) restarts with a systematic 'Récapitulation sur les groupes M_{ρ,σ}'.
